<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Burma Independence Army</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1941">1941</a>–<a href="/years/1942">1942</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> Independence Army was a nationalist force formed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a> in December 1941 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aung-san">Aung San</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-comrades">Thirty Comrades</a> with Japanese support. It advanced into Burma alongside the Japanese invasion of 1942, growing rapidly, and was reorganised by the Japanese as the Burma Defence Army and later the Burma National Army, which turned against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in March 1945. Myanmar's armed forces trace their origin to it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/burma-independence-army.jpg" alt="Burma Independence Army" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Havsjö · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:First_flag_of_the_Burma_Independence_Army.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Burmese nationalism grew under British rule, which began in 1824, and the Hsaya Rebellion of 1930–1932 was followed by organised anti-colonial politics. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aung-san">Aung San</a>, a student leader who joined the Dohbama Asiayone, the Thakins, in 1938, went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 1940 to seek support, and at Amoy he was detained by Colonel Keiji Suzuki, who had drawn up a plan for clandestine operations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>. In February 1941 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> formed the Minami Kikan to support Burmese resistance and close the Burma Road to China. Aung San and 29 others, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-comrades">Thirty Comrades</a>, left Burma in April 1941 and trained on Hainan Island.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>At the founding ceremony in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a> on 28 December 1941 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-comrades">Thirty Comrades</a> and Suzuki drank liquor mixed with their own blood, the thway thauk, pledging loyalty to the cause of independence. The army began with 227 Burmese and 74 Japanese, with Suzuki as commander-in-chief and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aung-san">Aung San</a> as senior staff officer, and entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> with 2,300 men in the invasion of January 1942. Volunteers swelled it to 10,000–12,000 by the time the Japanese reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangon">Rangoon</a> on 8 March, and eventually to between 18,000 and 23,000, armed largely with abandoned or captured British weapons.</p><p>Its men attacked minority populations, above all the Karens, and preyed on Indian refugees; in the Irrawaddy Delta elements of the army destroyed 400 Karen villages, with a death toll reaching 1,800. At Shwedaung on 29 March 1942 its forces under Bo Yan Naing fought beside a Japanese battalion and ambushed a retreating British force, losing 60 killed, 300 wounded, 60 captured and 350 missing. Its attempts to set up local governments led the Japanese to recall Suzuki and disband it on 24 July, forming the Burma Defence Army of 3,000 men under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aung-san">Aung San</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In 1943 its successor became the Burma National Army of the State of Burma under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ba-maw">Ba Maw</a>, with Bo Ne Win as commander-in-chief. Through the Anti-Fascist Organisation its leaders made contact with the British Force 136, and on 27 March 1945 they rose against the Japanese; the day is now Armed Forces Day in Myanmar. After talks at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandy">Kandy</a> in September 1945 only a small number of its men entered the new Burma Army, and many veterans joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aung-san">Aung San</a>'s People's Volunteer Organisation. Aung San and many of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-comrades">Thirty Comrades</a> are honoured in Burma as national heroes.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Burma Independence Army” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The armed forces, known as the Tatmadaw, trace their origin to the <b>Burma Independence Army</b> formed by Aung San with Japanese help in 1941, and their history since 1948 has been one of almost uninterrupted internal war.</blockquote><a href="/empire/myanmar">Myanmar · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Japan invaded British Burma in 1942 with the help of the <b>Burma Independence Army</b>, formed by Aung San and the Thirty Comrades.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-burma">State of Burma</a></li></ul></section>
